Flux

Two ways the oil and gas industry puts carbon into the air — gas it burns and
gas it leaks — on one map, under one date window.

  make serve     # static server on :8000 + signaling on :4444
  make test
  make vendor    # re-vendor cartograph + the s2e wasm core

Three detection layers, all drawn at once, one quarter grid over all of them:

  - S2: Sentinel-2 flare clusters from the data-desk tables in the Data Desk
    CloudFerro archive, read through Cartograph's DuckDB-Wasm layer.
  - Detect (outside archive coverage): downloads Sentinel-2 L2A COGs from the
    Element84 STAC catalogue and runs the detector in a Web Worker; connected
    peers split the work over WebRTC and merge results in a CRDT.
  - VNF: VIIRS Nightfire, from the eog tables in the same archive, which sees
    flares at night and measures their radiant heat.
  - Methane: dated plume observations from Carbon Mapper, IMEO, SRON and Data
    Desk, each carrying a measured release rate.

Shape says whether a feature burned or leaked, colour says only how much. The
two flaring instruments read on scales that are not comparable, so the key
states each one's units and its rows are that layer's colour bands: turn a
band off to filter it out, turn them all off to take the layer off the map.

Where things live:

  web/config.js      the map config, and the orchestration around it
  web/layers.js      marking, ramp and colour policy for every layer
  web/card/          one card header, one body per feature kind
  web/nearby.js      the "also here" row across the families
  web/flaring/       S2 archive + VNF readers, clustering, the local detector
  web/methane/       plume reader, attribution, candidates, licences, overlay
  web/vendor/        cartograph, the dd design system, DuckDB, MapLibre

The detector, clusterer and quality score are the s2-flares methodology core
(github.com/data-desk-eco/s2-flares), Rust compiled to wasm and vendored in
web/flaring/s2/ — the in-browser path produces the same results as the archive
run. The app is a cartograph consumer (github.com/data-desk-eco/cartograph —
the map system itself is documented there): web/config.js is the config passed
to mount(), and the flux-specific behaviour lives in the hook modules it wires
in. CLAUDE.md carries the invariants.

Zero npm dependencies: MapLibre GL, geotiff.js, DuckDB-Wasm and the wasm core
are vendored. The rest uses browser and Node.js built-ins. Production signaling
is a Cloudflare Durable Object (signal/worker.js).

References

Faruolo et al. (2024) The DAFI v2 algorithm for gas flare detection
https://doi.org/10.1088/1748-9326/ad82fb
Elvidge et al. (2013) VIIRS Nightfire: satellite pyrometry at night
https://doi.org/10.3390/rs5094423
Jacob et al. (2022) Quantifying methane emissions from the global scale down to
point sources
https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-22-9617-2022
